{
  "language": "javascript",
  "case": "sequelize",
  "why": "Sequelize as its own example app writes it: `sequelize.define` inside a function the connection is handed to, a class extending Sequelize's `Model` with columns from `init`, and associations in a separate file on locals destructured from the models registry \u2014 a file that never imports `sequelize` itself. REFERENCES follows the foreign key, and each association kind has an edge of its own. The model reached through an import has a *different name at every level* \u2014 the class `Musician`, the model `musician` (from `modelName`), and the local `Player` \u2014 so reading any of the three as the model's name fails the case. `seed.js` holds three `define` calls in a file that imports `sequelize` and none is a model: an untyped map, and two values rooted at Sequelize (`Op.is`, `Sequelize.NOW`) that are not column types. Columns use the parameterized `STRING(80)` form too. `booking.js` holds `gig`, a MySQL-style model keyed by two foreign keys whose every column is a chained modifier (`INTEGER.UNSIGNED`, `BIGINT.UNSIGNED.ZEROFILL`) \u2014 a type is anywhere in the chain, not only at its end \u2014 and it is imported by the name of the *variable* that holds it (`{ Booking }`), which is neither the model name (`gig`) nor any case of it. The fifth review pass. `booking.js` now holds a second model, `tour`, declared *first*, so a rule that takes a module's only or first model instead of the imported binding fails. `models/index.js` is the hand-written `db` index, a define written onto a local object that becomes the exports (`db.Post = define(\u2026)`); `tag.js` writes onto an express-style alias (`var api = exports = module.exports = {}`), and `comment.js` defines inside the exported literal. Each is a true association lost when only `exports.X` writes were recorded. Three refusals pin the fallbacks: an `exports.Story` write that a later `module.exports` replaces, a `Draft` defined inside a function and shadowed at the top level by the one actually exported, and a destructured `{ Musician }` of a module whose class is its default.",
